.TH "OSTRYA\-SERVE" "1" "" "ostrya" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ostrya\-serve \- Serve the repository over HTTP as an archive repository for pull
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
ostrya serve [OPTIONS]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Serve the repository over HTTP as an archive repository for pull.
.PP
The repository can be in any mode that ostrya reads. In a mode other than \fB\%archive\fR, the server builds each \fB\%\&.filez\fR object on request. The server also answers the ref listing at \fB\%/_ostrya/refs/v1\fR\&. Without \%\-\-read\-only, the server also has the receive endpoint of a push, and the command needs a method of push authentication. At start, the command reads and checks the client groups and the server group of the repository config. Without \%\-\-read\-only, it also reads the trust groups and the key groups. A bad group ends the command with status \%1. The endpoint selects no client group, so it opens no push session: each session request that passes the authentication gets \%403.
.PP
When all listeners are bound, the command writes the URL of each listener to standard output, one line each. Then it serves until the process ends. For each push session that commits, the command writes one \fB\%warning: STEP: MESSAGE\fR line to standard error for each warning of the session.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-listen\fR \fIADDR:PORT\fR
Listen on \%ADDR:PORT, an IP address and a port.
.IP
Give the option once for each address. Without it, the server listens on \fB\%127.0.0.1:8080\fR\&. Port \%0 lets the kernel choose a port. The command refuses a host name. When all listeners are bound, the command writes the URL of each listener to standard output, one line each.
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\-cert\fR \fIFILE\fR
Serve HTTPS with the certificate chain in FILE, in PEM.
.IP
The option needs \%\-\-tls\-key. ALPN selects \%HTTP/2 or \%HTTP/1.1. The command reads FILE once, at start, and refuses a file larger than \%1 MiB.
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR
The private key of \%\-\-tls\-cert, in PEM.
.IP
The command refuses an encrypted key, because it has no option for a passphrase. It reads FILE once, at start, and refuses a file larger than \%1 MiB.
.TP
\fB\-\-client\-ca\fR \fIFILE\fR
Verify client certificates against the CA certificates in FILE, in PEM.
.IP
The option needs \%\-\-tls\-cert. A client that presents a certificate must present one that the CA signed. A client that presents no certificate is served. A client certificate that the CA signed is a method of push authentication. The start checks of the client groups take the client CA from this option alone, also when \fB\%[ex\-ostrya server]\fR sets \fB\%tls\-client\-ca\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-read\-only\fR
Serve the files of a pull alone, with no receive endpoint.
.IP
Without this switch, the command needs a method of push authentication: \%\-\-push\-credentials, \%\-\-client\-ca, or \%\-\-allow\-anonymous\-push. With none of them, the command exits with status \%1 before it opens the repository. The options of the receive endpoint conflict with this switch.
.TP
\fB\-\-push\-credentials\fR \fIFILE\fR
Take the push credentials in FILE, one \%NAME:HEX line each.
.IP
HEX is the \%SHA\-256 digest of the token in \%64 lowercase hex digits. The command skips an empty line and a line that starts with \fB\%#\fR, and a malformed line stops the command at start. A bearer token passes the authentication if its digest matches a line. A Basic credential \%NAME:TOKEN passes if the digest of TOKEN matches the line of NAME. A request that passes opens no push session and gets \%403. Without \%\-\-tls\-cert, the option needs \%\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials or \%\-\-allow\-anonymous\-push. The command reads FILE once, at start, and refuses a file larger than \%1 MiB.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials\fR
Take a bearer or Basic credential of push over plain HTTP.
.IP
Without this switch, the receive endpoint answers \%403 to such a request, also with \%\-\-allow\-anonymous\-push. Use it for a server on a loopback address or behind a proxy that terminates TLS. A read of the files of a pull needs no credential.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-anonymous\-push\fR
Let a request with no credential pass the authentication of a push.
.IP
A request that passes opens no push session and gets \%403.
.TP
\fB\-\-session\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR
Abort a push session that stays idle for SECONDS.
.IP
A push session is idle when it has no request in progress, or when its request body delivers no byte. The value is a positive integer. Default: \%300.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-sessions\fR \fIN\fR
Serve at most N push sessions at the same time.
.IP
The value is a positive integer. A request that opens a session past this limit gets the HTTP status \%503. Default: \%16.
.TP
\fB\-\-parallel\-uploads\fR \fIN\fR
Let a push session send N object streams at the same time.
.IP
The value is \%1 to \%31. The \fB\%HelloReply\fR of a session announces it. Over \%HTTP/2, the receive window of a stream is \%2 MiB, and the window of a connection is N times \%2 MiB. Default: \%4.
.TP
\fB\-\-body\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR
End a connection whose response waits more than SECONDS for the client.
.IP
The response waits for the client to take its next bytes. If an \%HTTP/2 client does not answer a ping within SECONDS, it also loses its connection. The value is a positive integer. The option also applies with \%\-\-read\-only. Default: \%60.
.PP
The global options are listed in \fBostrya\fR(1).
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBostrya\fR(1)
